MEM-001,Memory & Retrieval,Selective Retrieval,Keep active memory small; durable shared knowledge lives here. Use indexes/cues to retrieve relevant source-backed records; avoid recursive summary compression.,Cross-project architecture finding,validated,2026-10-01
MEM-002,Memory & Retrieval,Retrieve–Place–Bind,"When a concept surfaces from memory, first place it into the current conversational sequence with its referent intact, then use or bind its modifiers, predicates, comparisons, and conditions. Retrieve → Place → Bind preserves temporal precedence and prevents attributes from migrating to a later semantically similar concept. Treat violations as high-severity conversational-integrity failures because they can preserve fluent language while corrupting meaning.","Fire Form Kata referent-binding incident; user-derived correction, 2026-10-15",validated,2026-10-15
MEM-003,Agent Architecture,Low-Friction Reliability Rules,Prefer the smallest reliable constraint that improves a process the model already performs natively. Avoid parallel supervisory frameworks when a simple tweak to the natural path will work; future threads are more likely to execute a low-friction rule than maintain duplicative bookkeeping.,"Cross-project architecture discussion, 2026-10-15",validated,2026-10-15
MEM-004,Memory & Retrieval,Recover Before Reporting Failure,"Treat a failed link or tool read as an access-path failure, not proof the requested information is absent. Investigate cause, retry or switch to alternate authorized sources and routes, verify the data, and record successful workarounds with their limits. Report an unresolved gap only after reasonable recovery, with useful diagnostics. Prefer small low-friction retrieval improvements.",User directive 2026-11-03; Rem Scratchpad; 2026-11-04 observed Sheets 429 recovered via Drive fetch and Gmail intermittent reads recovered by targeted retries.,validated,2026-11-04